        "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs with chronic urinary tract infection (UTI)s or urolithiasis.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "No cases reported in cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Initially, may be asymptomatic.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hematuria is the most common sign.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gross hematuria often occurs at the end of the micturition stream during maximal bladder contraction.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pollakiuria and dysuria may also be present and are associated with irritation of the polyps.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urethral obstruction could occur if a sufficient number of bladder polyps are located in the trigone of the bladder. Polyps originating or in the urethra may also cause partial or total obstruction.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ureteral obstruction may occur if polyps surround the ureteral orifice.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "UTI may be present concurrently, which can predispose to upper UTI.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Causes of polypoid cystitis in dogs have not been well documented, but this disorder is commonly associated with UTI, urolithiasis, and other types of chronic irritation.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Chronic infection and inflammation secondary to long-term indwelling transurethral urinary catheters and urinary stents have been associated with polypoid cystitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Polypoid cystitis should be ruled out in cases of hematuria, pollakiuria, dysuria, and recurrent UTI.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bladder neoplasms (such as TCC), UTI, and urolithiasis are the most common rule-outs.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Serum biochemistry profiles should be normal in most cases of polypoid cystitis unless azotemia occurs as a result of obstruction to urine outflow and/or concurrent pyelonephritis.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinalysis may reveal hematuria, pyuria, and transitional epithelial cells.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urine should be cultured by clean midstream collection, clean catheterization or at the time of cystoscopy. Culture by cystocentesis is also appropriate once TCC has been ruled out. This will prevent potential spread of TCC along the needle track.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Representative polyps removed via cysto scopy or cystotomy should be preserved in formalin for light microscopic examination.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "An adequate sample of bladder tissue should be placed in culture media suitable for growth of microbes, preferably prior to antibiotic administration.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Survey radiographs may reveal a normal bladder, an irregular thickening of the surface contour of the bladder, and/or concurrent urolithiasis.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Double-contrast cystography or positivecontrast cystography may reveal irregular polypoid fronds projecting in the bladder lumen and/or a thickened bladder wall. The most common location of involvement is the cranioventral bladder wall. Single large polyps with a narrow or broad base may be observed scattered throughout the contrast puddle. Uroliths will migrate to the most dependent portion of the contrast puddle.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ultrasonography may show polyps scattered along the mucosal surface of the bladder, especially in the cranioventral aspect.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cystoscopic or ultrasound-guided biopsy may be used to obtain representative portions of the polyps for light microscopic exam ination of stained sections of the lesions.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "More invasive, full-thickness biopsy of the wall of the bladder via cystotomy may be required to include the base of the polyps with an adequate margin of healthy tissue.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gross changes involving the mucosal surface of the urinary bladder include single or multiple small masses ranging from 1 to 10 mm in size. The masses may be nodular with a broad base or attached to the bladder wall by a thin stalk.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Light microscopic changes include polypoid projections of hyperplastic epithelium that surround a core of proliferative connective tissue mixed with acute and chronic inflammation (congestion, edema, and acute and chronic inflammatory cells). Findings characteristic of malignancy (e.g., disproportionate enlargement of nuclei in relation to the cytoplasm; increase in chromatin content causing hyperchromasia;",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "structural changes such as aberrant chromatin patterns, lack of distinct cell boundaries, irregularity in cell outlines; enlargement and/or increase in number of nuclei; multinucleated cells with atypical nuclei; abnormal number of mitotic figures; cytoplasmic inclusions and vacuolization; invasion of basement membranes) are conspicuously absent. Eosinophils have been present; their significance is unknown. In some patients, changes typical of granulomatous inflammation predominate.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization identifies bacteria when urine and tissue culture was negative.",
              "pdf_page": 1193,
              "printed_page": 1111,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Elimination of bacteria that are a source of chronic irritation is a rational therapeutic goal.",
          "pdf_page": 1193,
          "printed_page": 1111,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Nonsurgical removal of urocystoliths (voiding urohydropropulsion, lithotripsy, dietary management) that are causing chronic irritation may result in elimination of inflammatory polyps.",
          "pdf_page": 1193,
          "printed_page": 1111,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The polyps can be removed nonsurgically (stone basket extraction, laser ablation, electrocautery via cystoscopy or surgically via cystotomy).",
          "pdf_page": 1193,
          "printed_page": 1111,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Partial cystectomy may be required to remove polyps affecting large portions of the bladder. The remaining portion of the urinary bladder may return to normal size in 3–6 months.",
          "pdf_page": 1193,
          "printed_page": 1111,
          "column": 3
        }
      ],
